from lessons import L
L(11, 'Lists: a row of boxes', '📋', 30,
  'Store many values in one list, change it with methods, loop over it, and avoid the classic list traps.',
  ['I can make a list and read items by index (from 0 or from -1)',
   'I can add, remove, sort and count items with list methods',
   'I can loop over a list and build one with append',
   'I know b = a does not copy a list, and that sort() returns None'],
  [
  ('p', 'You want to store the scores of 30 students. Do you make 30 variables? No! A <b>list</b> is <b>one variable that holds a whole row of numbered boxes</b>. You make one with square brackets and commas.'),
  ('try', 'scores = [90, 85, 77]\nprint(scores)\nprint(len(scores))', [], 'Run it. <code>print</code> shows the brackets and commas too. <code>len</code> counts the boxes. Then add a fourth score and run again.'),
  ('rule', 'Boxes are numbered from 0', 'The number of a box is its <b>index</b>. The <b>first</b> box is index <b>0</b>, not 1. A negative index counts from the end: <code>-1</code> is the <b>last</b> box. Indexing and slicing work <b>exactly like on strings</b>, and a slice of a list is a list.'),
  ('show', 'names = ["Ana", "Bo", "Cy", "Di"]\nprint(names[0])\nprint(names[-1])\nprint(names[1:3])\nprint(names[:2])\nprint(len(names))', []),
  ('trap', '<b>The last index is <code>len(list) - 1</code>.</b> A list with 3 boxes has indexes 0, 1, 2. Asking for <code>scores[3]</code> crashes with an <b>IndexError</b>. This off-by-one is the number 1 list bug.'),
  ('h', 'Lists can be changed (strings cannot)'),
  ('try', 'scores = [90, 85, 77]\nscores[1] = 100\nprint(scores)\nword = "cat"\nword[0] = "b"', [], 'The list line works: you can put a new value in a box. The last line crashes: a <b>string cannot be changed</b>, but a <b>list can</b>.'),
  ('h', 'List methods and tools'),
  ('table', ['Code', 'What it does'], [
     ['<code>a.append(x)</code>', 'add x at the <b>end</b>'],
     ['<code>a.insert(i, x)</code>', 'put x at index i, push the rest along'],
     ['<code>a.remove(x)</code>', 'delete the <b>first</b> x it finds (ValueError if missing)'],
     ['<code>a.pop()</code> / <code>a.pop(i)</code>', 'delete the last / index i item <b>and give it back</b>'],
     ['<code>a.sort()</code> / <code>a.reverse()</code>', 'put in order / flip the list (changes the list itself)'],
     ['<code>a.index(x)</code> / <code>a.count(x)</code>', 'position of the first x / how many x'],
     ['<code>a.extend(b)</code>', 'add every item of list b at the end'],
     ['<code>sum(a)</code> <code>min(a)</code> <code>max(a)</code> <code>sorted(a)</code>', 'total / smallest / biggest / a NEW sorted list'],
     ['<code>x in a</code>', 'True if x is one of the items'],
     ['<code>a + b</code> and <code>a * 3</code>', 'join two lists / repeat a list']]),
  ('try', 'a = [5, 2, 9]\na.append(7)\na.insert(0, 1)\nprint(a)\nlast = a.pop()\nprint(last)\na.sort()\nprint(a)\nprint(sum(a), min(a), max(a))\nprint(9 in a)', [], 'Predict every line first. Then Run and check. Notice that <code>pop()</code> gives you the removed item.'),
  ('trap', '<b>sort() returns None.</b> It sorts the list <i>in place</i> and gives back nothing. So <code>x = a.sort()</code> makes x equal to <code>None</code>, and <code>a = a.sort()</code> destroys your list. Write <code>a.sort()</code> alone, or use <code>b = sorted(a)</code> to get a new list. The same is true of <code>append</code>, <code>insert</code>, <code>remove</code> and <code>reverse</code>.'),
  ('h', 'Loops over lists'),
  ('p', '<code>for item in scores:</code> gives you each <b>value</b>, one at a time. It is the easiest way to total things up or count things.'),
  ('trace', 'scores = [90, 75, 82]\ntotal = 0\nbig = 0\nfor s in scores:\n    total += s\n    if s > 80:\n        big += 1\nprint(total, big)', []),
  ('p', 'If you need the <b>position</b> too (or want to change items), loop over the indexes with <code>for i in range(len(scores)):</code> and use <code>scores[i]</code>.'),
  ('try', 'nums = [1, 2, 3]\nfor i in range(len(nums)):\n    nums[i] = nums[i] * 10\nprint(nums)\nfor n in nums:\n    n = n + 1\nprint(nums)', [], 'The first loop changes the list. The second does NOT: <code>n</code> is only a <b>copy</b> of each item, so changing n leaves the list alone. To change items, write into <code>nums[i]</code>.'),
  ('h', 'Building a list'),
  ('p', 'Start with an <b>empty list</b> <code>[]</code>, then add to it inside a loop with <code>append</code>. Convert with <code>int()</code> BEFORE appending if you want numbers.'),
  ('try', 'nums = []\nfor i in range(3):\n    nums.append(int(input()))\nprint(nums)\nprint(sum(nums) / len(nums))', ['4', '5', '9'], 'Type three numbers. The list grows by one each time round the loop, then we print it and its average.'),
  ('rule', 'The bridge to strings: split and join', '<code>text.split()</code> cuts a string at the spaces and gives you a <b>list of words</b>. <code>" ".join(words)</code> glues a list of strings back into <b>one string</b> (the text in quotes goes between the items). <code>list(range(5))</code> makes <code>[0, 1, 2, 3, 4]</code>.'),
  ('show', 'words = "to be or not".split()\nprint(words)\nprint(len(words))\nprint("-".join(words))\nprint(list(range(5)))', []),
  ('h', 'The aliasing trap: one list, two names'),
  ('trap', '<code>b = a</code> does <b>not</b> copy a list. It gives the SAME list a second name. Change it through either name and both names see the change. To get a real copy write <code>b = a.copy()</code>.'),
  ('show', 'a = [1, 2, 3]\nb = a\nb.append(4)\nprint(a)\nc = [1, 2, 3]\nd = c.copy()\nd.append(4)\nprint(c)\nprint(d)', []),
  ('h', 'Mistakes and the error each one causes'),
  ('table', ['Mistake', 'What happens'], [
     ['<code>scores[3]</code> on a 3-item list', 'IndexError (the last index is 2)'],
     ['<code>for i in range(len(a) + 1): a[i]</code>', 'IndexError on the last round (off by one)'],
     ['<code>a.remove(50)</code> when 50 is not in a', 'ValueError'],
     ['<code>a + 5</code> (list + number)', 'TypeError (use <code>a + [5]</code> or <code>a.append(5)</code>)'],
     ['<code>sum(["3", "4"])</code> (items are text)', 'TypeError (convert with <code>int()</code> first)'],
     ['<code>a = a.sort()</code> then <code>a.append(1)</code>', 'AttributeError: a is None now, and None has no append'],
     ['<code>word[0] = "z"</code> on a string', 'TypeError (strings cannot change)']]),
  ('quick', 'a = [10, 20, 30]. What does print(a[-1]) show?', ['30', '10', '-1', 'Error'], 0, '-1 is the last box, which holds 30.'),
  ('quick', 'a = [3, 1, 2] then x = a.sort(). What is x?', ['None', '[1, 2, 3]', '[3, 1, 2]', '3'], 0, 'sort() changes a but returns None.'),
  ('quick', 'a = [1, 2] and b = a and b.append(3). What is a?', ['[1, 2, 3]', '[1, 2]', '[3]', 'Error'], 0, 'a and b are the same list, so the 3 shows up in both.'),
  ])
